00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:21.380
What we now go
and take a look at

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.510
is if you are
trying to run Active

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.800
Directory or
AD-related apps on GCP,

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.970
how do you go ahead and do that?

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:32.750
And for this, we do not
need Cloud identity or a VPN

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:36.030
to be set up for the things
we're going to talk about next.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.086
So that's something.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:44.450
So a lot of times when you need
Active Directory in the cloud

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.490
is if you were thinking
of a migration project.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:48.590
So go ahead and soak in
that visual for a second,

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:49.631
and I'll talk through it.

00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:58.780
So what you're seeing is you
have an application that's

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:00.196
talking to your
on-premises Active

00:10:00.196 --> 00:10:03.530
Directory over a set of
Active Directory protocols.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:06.030
Now, what are the cases when
you may need Active Directory

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.970
as you think of moving this
app or this server onto GCP?

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:11.526
So one very common
use case is if you

00:10:11.526 --> 00:10:12.900
have gone ahead
and domain-joined

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.720
these applications onto
Google Cloud Platform--

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.700
onto Active Directory,
and you want to run them

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:18.741
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:18.741 --> 00:10:20.840
So in that case,
you're looking for AD.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:24.200
In other cases, if the app does
LDAP, or Kerberos, or Windows

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:27.490
Integrated Auth, it's looking
for a domain controller.

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:29.010
The next set of
things is if the app

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:30.930
has logic for
retrieving things that

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.330
are stored in Active
Directory itself,

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.460
most often it's
group-based membership when

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:38.970
the application houses
authorization logic in itself.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:42.720
Another time is if you had
used an AD client-side library.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.180
For instance, you
had a .NET-based app.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.890
And if that was
making any calls over

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.020
to System.DirectoryServices,
it would look

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.250
to find a domain controller.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:53.470
And the last bit is, when
you do go ahead and have

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:55.300
your application
migrated to the cloud,

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.850
you have a choice to make,
which is do you still

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.430
want your on-premise network
to be on the critical path

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.670
for each application
authentication look up?

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:05.710
Or would you rather
have it scale in cloud,

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:08.080
have low latency,
and have the cloud

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.990
application be independently
and highly available.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:13.470
So how is it that these
choices end up manifesting

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:14.690
your architecture options?

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:17.490
Let's take a look.

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:20.490
First thing you can do in any
of these cases is the simplest,

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:24.570
just go ahead and set up your
VPN or a Cloud Interconnect

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:28.104
and have your Active Directory
continue to recite on-premises.

00:11:28.104 --> 00:11:29.520
And so this way,
it's pretty quick

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.680
to configure but you have
higher latency for all

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.120
your applications.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.680
Every single request
is going back

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:37.060
to your on-premises network.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:39.590
So what do you do?

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:41.340
Second option you have
is you can go ahead

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.260
and choose to deploy
read-only domain

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.870
controllers onto Google Cloud.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:47.630
Now, the advantage
in this case is

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:49.672
it's a read-only replica
of Active Directory.

00:11:49.672 --> 00:11:51.380
But those are you who
have worked with it

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:53.510
for a while would
probably know that there

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:56.210
are certain operations that are
still being chained or referred

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:58.940
back to your full domain
controllers, which in this case

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:00.500
would reside on-premises.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:03.140
So you're absolutely going back
to your on-premises network

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:05.330
for getting those done.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:07.100
In terms of a
positive, this does not

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:10.220
bring in everybody's credentials
onto any cloud deployments.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:12.470
So you can have no
users' passwords

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:14.780
in the cloud by
default. Some of you

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:17.180
may like that from a
security perspective.

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:20.270
However, it also lets
you only have replicated

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:22.100
in a certain set
of users' passwords

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:24.990
into that cloud AD deployment.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.510
On the other hand, the
biggest challenge with RODCs

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:29.250
is there's application
compatibility

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:30.750
things you should think about.

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:33.925
So if you go in Google for
RODC application compatibility,

00:12:33.925 --> 00:12:35.550
the first link gives
you a list of apps

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:37.160
that support RODCs or not.

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.840
So that's something
to factor in.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.320
The next option is to go ahead
and deploy your writeable

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.550
or regular domain
controllers into cloud.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.550
And in this case, you obviously
have your Active Directory data

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:49.770
available entirely on GCP.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.230
It's low latency because
your application is then

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:56.270
going for all operations to
the ADDC you just apply there.

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:58.410
However, the thing to
keep in mind in both this

00:12:58.410 --> 00:12:59.785
as well as the
previous configure

00:12:59.785 --> 00:13:02.751
is if you have multiple
Active Directory domains,

00:13:02.751 --> 00:13:05.250
you may end up needing to deploy
domain controllers for each

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.270
of those domains into cloud.

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:09.920
So if you had a merger
and acquisition use case

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:12.540
or for any other reasons had
three or four AD domains,

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:15.000
[INAUDIBLE] multiple domain
controllers from each of those

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.310
domains.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:18.860
So to get around that,
another thing you can do

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:22.270
is set up a new Active
Directory domain on Google Cloud

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:25.490
Platform, use the Active
Directory trust relationship

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:29.850
model to establish a trust
with your on-premise AD.

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:32.310
And this does work well
because you effectively

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:34.740
are able to have three or four
different AD domains where

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:37.140
our users are having
their credentials stored

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:38.700
and authentication happening.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:41.130
However, all your resources
and applications, which

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:42.630
are running in the
cloud, can join

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:45.840
or just work against the Active
Directory domain which is now

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.564
hosted fully within Cloud.

00:13:48.564 --> 00:13:50.230
Another thing you
could think about when

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:52.900
you think of moving to the cloud
and having a hybrid deployment

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:56.410
is to consider having a disaster
recovery site on Google Cloud

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:57.540
Platform.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:00.130
And so the numbers you're
seeing on the right

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:03.400
effectively show an example
of AD site replication

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:07.360
costs, which you could utilize
to go ahead and have things set

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:12.190
up so that your users and
applications on-premises

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:14.500
are usually not reaching
back to the cloud.

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:16.060
And only in the
event of a failover

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.370
will they come in and
use the cloud DCs.

00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:20.974
So these are five options you
have in terms of architecture.

00:14:20.974 --> 00:14:22.390
And by this point,
you're probably

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:25.480
wondering, which of these makes
sense for your specific use

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:26.900
case?

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:28.540
Now, to help answer
that, I'm really

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:31.150
excited to share with you
the highlight of our segment

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:33.790
today, which is the
Ben &amp; Kenny experience.

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.410
They'll come up
and share with you

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:37.090
what is it that worked
for them as they

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:39.570
look to have AD running on GCP.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:43.134
KENNY HILL: Hi again, guys.

00:14:43.134 --> 00:14:44.550
First, I want to
thank Sid, I want

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.310
to thank Ben for allowing
me to come up here and share

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:51.030
a little bit about Capital One's
Google Cloud Active Directory

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:53.670
journey.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.670
Hopefully there's time
at the end for questions.

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:57.220
If not, feel free to connect.

00:14:57.220 --> 00:14:58.810
Always really
excited to hear what

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:01.120
others are doing in the
identity and directory

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:03.190
space in the cloud.

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:05.470
See the question up on
the board-- or screen.

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:08.830
Why extend Active
Directory to Google Cloud?

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:10.690
There are many reasons.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:15.460
The main driver for us
is resiliency and speed.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:17.950
Let me explain a little
bit of what that means

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:21.100
and some of the design
inputs that went

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:22.420
into some of our decisions.

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:28.710
There are four main areas
we focus on with our domain

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:30.430
controller design.

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:32.290
First one, security.

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:34.210
I'm going to gloss
over that and skip it.

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:36.250
Not enough time for me
to really dive deep.

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:40.520
Ben is going to share
some security practices.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:43.990
Next area for us
is directory size.

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.150
Since going on on
the cloud, we've

00:15:46.150 --> 00:15:49.730
had exponential growth
in our directories,

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:52.260
running at a 38 and 60 gigabit.

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:59.500
NTDS did have about 2.5 million
objects in each directory,

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:04.240
between 100,000, 150,000
both users and group objects.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:13.110
Understanding
directory size, there's

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:15.180
many reasons for our growth.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:19.440
One of the main ones
that I'm excited about

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:23.850
is the development we've had on
Cloud Platform of applications

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:25.630
requiring role-based access.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:30.040
We've also taken the
concept of tags from cloud,

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:32.500
and we're writing more
data into our directory.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:37.080
This is allows us to correlate
Active Directory objects

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:40.320
with Cloud things utilizing
our provisioning system.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:46.070
Next, there is a resiliency.

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:47.260
The next is speed.

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:48.830
As I was doing the
slides for this,

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:53.810
I realized same inputs
really went into both of them

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:57.860
to ensure Active
Directory authentication

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:00.570
and authorization
is always available.

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.770
Doesn't matter if it's a
federated SAML authentication,

00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:08.780
a Windows authentication, LDAP
Application bind, a database

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:12.050
authentication, the
directory needs to be there.

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:15.560
Same holds true when your
domain-joining infrastructure,

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:16.980
and it wants to scale.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.810
So the directory
needs to be there.

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:22.099
So for us, going down
Interconnect or VPN, that

00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:24.859
wasn't really an
option as we want to,

00:17:24.859 --> 00:17:29.390
no matter the situation, ensure
that we can scale applications.

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:37.670
To create a seamless
experience for applications

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:42.020
when switching projects
in regions, a developer,

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:45.680
application team, DevOps team,
no matter what you call them,

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:49.010
they should not need to know
anything about AD sites,

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:52.130
maintain different URL mappings.

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:55.800
We even have application
teams that they don't even

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.560
know it's Active
Directory on the backend.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:59.726
When they need help, they
ask where's the LDAP team?

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:05.310
To provide the fastest
domain-join directory

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:09.100
replication and LDAP
experience possible.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:11.490
A key way to be
successful in this bullet

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:16.984
is to keeping your
traffic on Google Cloud.

00:18:16.984 --> 00:18:18.900
I'm not going to give
exact time test metrics.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:22.240
You guys, I highly recommend
testing this yourself.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.310
In a world where every
millisecond counts

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:32.820
it is important to
ensure that traffic

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:35.040
stays on the most optimal path.

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:43.260
And this is probably one
of the biggest reasons

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:48.970
we build domain controllers
on Compute Engine,

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:53.000
to quickly rehydrate and be
able to increase AD capacity.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:56.830
At Capital One, we
rebuild all infrastructure

00:18:56.830 --> 00:19:00.260
every 45 to 60 days maximum.

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:02.670
Domain controllers are no
exception to that rule.

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:05.030
They get rebuilt. We
call this rehydrate.

00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:10.530
So we are constantly rebuilding
our domain controllers.

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:13.856
That's why directory
size is up there.

00:19:13.856 --> 00:19:17.270
You need to understand
your build times.

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:19.700
It's important to
know exactly how

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:22.940
long it's going to take to
build domain controllers.

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:25.970
Even if using the
install for media file,

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:30.230
which you back up the AD
database or SYSVOL you

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:32.090
import it.

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:35.600
If you have quite a
few objects, the domain

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:38.630
promote process is still
going to do a checksum--

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:41.840
I don't know the correct term
for it-- but of every object.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:43.730
And that's where the
lag's going to happen.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:48.140
So understanding your
directory size and the speed

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:49.850
that you can build
domain controllers is

00:19:49.850 --> 00:19:52.840
going to help you determine is
your directory's small enough?

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:55.550
Are you able to auto scale?

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:57.110
If so, I'm jealous.

00:19:57.110 --> 00:19:59.915
Or are you going to
have to run it, monitor

00:19:59.915 --> 00:20:02.660
it a little bit over your
monitoring high peaks,

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:05.810
meaning you're running a little
bit over capacity at all times,

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:10.760
or do you keep a reserve pool of
live domain controllers running

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:14.600
on smaller instances
in a reserved AD site

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:18.050
and utilize scaling
technology to bring them

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:20.094
in and out as you need them?

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:26.900
Next, I'm going to show you a
few of the high-level design

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:29.390
decisions.

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:34.760
In a traditional
AD site typology,

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:37.190
for on-premise you'd
usually have a single AD

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:39.980
site or two per data center.

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:41.840
Each of these AD
sites is usually

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:44.515
going to have some sort
of single point of failure

00:20:44.515 --> 00:20:47.090
if it's power, network,
shared storage,

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:48.350
or other infrastructure.

00:20:52.310 --> 00:20:56.360
In our Google Cloud
model, we utilize--

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:58.790
we put domain
controllers in every zone

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:01.260
available in each
region we deploy.

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:04.760
This is essentially
treating the whole region

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:06.100
as a single AD site.

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:18.710
This provides a more
resilient typology.

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:27.440
In the on-premise model it's
hard to test if you can-- you

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:30.430
manage your sites and
services, and your cost,

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:33.110
and you for the
next closest site,

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:35.369
and there can be errors in it.

00:21:35.369 --> 00:21:37.910
You can shut down network to a
data center, and you can say--

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:41.790
and you can know that
everything's authenticated

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:44.240
and authorization's
occurring, but do you

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:47.390
know exactly where
all that's going?

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:50.420
In this model in the
cloud, you know it's just

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:51.650
staying in the same site.

00:21:57.020 --> 00:22:00.890
This also ensures that any
applications that utilize DC

00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:06.500
locator process are going
to pick domain controller

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:09.440
in the AD site, in
region, staying on Google

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:13.010
Cloud as their primary.

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:16.950
For applications that
cannot utilize DC locator,

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:18.650
we utilize an LDAP
load balancer.

00:22:21.850 --> 00:22:27.650
Our configuration for load
balancer LDAP secure 636, GCS

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:32.480
global catalog secure 3269
coming into the load balancer.

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:37.120
This ensures that all traffic
outside of the project

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:39.400
is secure.

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:47.560
We then terminate at the load
balancer to regular 3893268

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:51.600
and point at domain controllers
across multiple zones.

00:22:57.040 --> 00:23:00.880
Realize global catalog
secure port for applications

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:03.010
which chase referrals.

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:05.890
Many Java-based applications
chase referrals.

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:09.490
And sometimes you have COTS
products deployed which you

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:10.510
cannot disable in.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.170
If you're not familiar
with chase referrals,

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:19.470
it's when you point an
application to a domain

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:22.230
controller, and instead
of utilizing that domain

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:24.410
controller, it pulls
back all the A records

00:23:24.410 --> 00:23:27.570
and randomizes
them per the client

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.280
and then just
picks a random one.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:31.650
If you utilize
global catalog port,

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:33.120
it disables referral chasing.

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:43.330
We're predominantly
a Linux shop,

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:48.280
which does not have DC
locator process by default

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:50.170
without third-party products.

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:54.880
There are also many applications
which are not AD site aware,

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:56.890
they cannot utilize
a load balancer,

00:23:56.890 --> 00:23:59.740
or even some of the horrible
applications that you have

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.260
to point directly to
a domain controller.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:03.970
An example of one of
those applications

00:24:03.970 --> 00:24:06.820
you'd point directly
at a domain controller

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:10.330
is something that
needs to read and write

00:24:10.330 --> 00:24:12.400
almost simultaneously,
like your identity

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:15.760
and lifecycle management
application or provisioning

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:18.850
application where if it doesn't
read and write at the same one,

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:22.300
you could have collisions.

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:25.990
We utilize a root DSE
query, so LDAPS route

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:31.700
DSE query to the load balancer
returning the DNS hostname.

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:34.240
The applications
can utilize this.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:36.010
They can put it in
a script or a job

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:38.890
and ensure their configuration
is always pointing

00:24:38.890 --> 00:24:40.450
at a live domain controller.

00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:43.450
Even if we're rebuilding
or rehydrating

00:24:43.450 --> 00:24:46.390
our domain controllers, if
they have this job set up,

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:48.810
they'll know they always have
a live domain controller.

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:55.220
As I mentioned, it is
extremely important

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:59.420
to ensure we are replicating
Active Directory data

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:01.520
as fast as possible
in this model.

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:03.750
If we were to fail
to another region,

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:07.130
applications need to be able
to read the same data that they

00:25:07.130 --> 00:25:09.500
read in one region
in another one.

00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:12.050
Also allows us to
more effectively get

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:15.710
to adjust in-time
access model and enhance

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:18.840
our privileged access
password rotation.

00:25:22.930 --> 00:25:28.270
The legacy replication strategy
was designed more than 18 years

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:34.010
ago when network was slow
and also very expensive.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:40.060
We choose to utilize a full
mass replication with change

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:41.320
notification strategy.

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:50.420
Change notification treats
replication on the site link

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:52.212
same as it would in our site.

00:25:52.212 --> 00:25:55.730
Though the diagram
I'm showing up there,

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:59.180
we are replicating from
site to site on-premise

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.930
to all of our Google regions.

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:05.600
And then we do,
as fast as we can,

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:07.240
replication between the sites.

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:13.810
To close out, a couple
more lessons that we've

00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:16.590
learned along the way.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.820
Limit your use of
group policy objects.

00:26:18.820 --> 00:26:21.850
There are many more
cloud-friendly configuration

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:23.920
management solutions out there.

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:26.770
Ben's going to actually show you
one that's available for free

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:28.580
that works.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:32.830
You can also utilize-- you
can also harden your image,

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:36.280
stack all your configuration
settings in your image.

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:39.820
And that's another approach.

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:45.220
Managing group policy deviations
and exceptions in a cloud model

00:26:45.220 --> 00:26:49.840
is not operationally efficient
for the directory support

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:51.850
teams or the application teams.

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:53.950
The application teams are
going to need to script

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:57.640
moving the objects or adding to
a security filter group, which

00:26:57.640 --> 00:27:01.360
means they need credentials,
which means they have

00:27:01.360 --> 00:27:02.860
to also have secure a password.

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:06.610
So we don't take that
approach, we only

00:27:06.610 --> 00:27:10.960
deploy GPO for high-level
settings, which there's

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:12.430
no way we're going to reverse.

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:17.390
Monitor and log everything.

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:22.400
Even if you're not going to
utilize the data immediately,

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:23.540
you will want it later.

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:32.240
There are many other services
besides Compute Engine

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:37.280
which can enhance directory
services in the cloud.

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:39.920
So you will want
this historical data

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:42.835
when you're building out tools
and applications on Google

00:27:42.835 --> 00:27:43.460
Cloud Platform.

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:50.530
Automate your domain
controller build.

00:27:50.530 --> 00:27:52.020
I already mentioned this one.

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:53.200
It's worth repeating.

00:27:57.560 --> 00:28:00.490
Ensure your build process
for domain controllers

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:04.150
is efficient, and you're
able to quickly add capacity.

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:06.670
Domain controllers are
in a different category

00:28:06.670 --> 00:28:09.190
of core services than
regular applications.

00:28:09.190 --> 00:28:11.290
The domain controllers
need to be there

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:13.900
before the applications
need to consume it.

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:18.170
Another thing is AD, of
you who are very familiar,

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:19.780
It's extremely chatty.

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:21.820
Wait until you put
it in the cloud.

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:24.820
When teens are auto-scaling
they're spinning up

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:27.980
1,000 instances at a
time, pulling them down,

00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:29.530
AD's to be chattier.

00:28:29.530 --> 00:28:33.160
So being able to add capacity
really quick is important.

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:34.780
Last thing I'll leave you with.

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:38.950
At Capital One, we do not
troubleshoot isolated domain

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:40.720
controller issues in the cloud.

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:42.550
We will give it one reboot.

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:46.120
If that issue persists,
domain controller comes out,

00:28:46.120 --> 00:28:47.470
we bring another one in.

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:49.926
Thank you guys for sitting
and listening to me.

00:28:49.926 --> 00:28:50.550
Thank you, Sid.

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:53.346
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:54.855 --> 00:28:56.230
SIDDHARTH BHAI:
That was actually

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:58.750
