00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:00.880
that you heard Capital
One talk about how they're

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:03.880
running Active Directory
in production on GCP

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:05.710
with a fairly large size.

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:08.654
The two angles in particular
that stood out for me was one

00:29:08.654 --> 00:29:10.570
is, even when you are
looking to run something

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:13.340
like Active Directory, which
is a very core Windows Server

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:17.500
workload trying to do LDAP, by
leveraging Cloud DNS and Cloud

00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:19.330
load balancing, it
was interesting to see

00:29:19.330 --> 00:29:21.880
how they designed the
LDAP load balancer.

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:23.800
That's definitely
something-- things like this

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:26.530
as you go out and explore
of moving to the cloud are

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:29.200
interesting ideas to try.

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:30.772
The second was the
message that we

00:29:30.772 --> 00:29:32.980
were left with which is,
you should go ahead and look

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:35.590
to automate your domain
controller creation,

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:37.360
the domain controller
build process.

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:38.980
And that's actually
a great point.

00:29:38.980 --> 00:29:40.780
And I'm excited to
call upon Ben who

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:43.510
is going to come in and share
with us more about what is it

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:46.300
that Alphabet does when
it also deploys domain

00:29:46.300 --> 00:29:47.800
controllers in production.

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:50.380
And how is it that you've
automated DC Build.

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:51.694
Welcome Ben.

00:29:51.694 --> 00:29:52.900
BENJAMIN MILLER: Hi.

00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:55.660
As I mentioned a bit ago, I work
on Google's M&amp;A Infrastructure

00:29:55.660 --> 00:29:56.920
Services Team.

00:29:56.920 --> 00:29:58.780
I work with a group
of talented systems

00:29:58.780 --> 00:30:00.940
administrators in
Colorado and California

00:30:00.940 --> 00:30:03.010
that built and operate
the project that I'm going

00:30:03.010 --> 00:30:04.093
to be talking about today.

00:30:06.470 --> 00:30:07.970
One of the things
that our team does

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:10.640
is to build and maintain
core it infrastructure

00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:13.370
for an array of acquisitions
and partnerships.

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:16.840
To make this happen, we rely
heavily on code and automation.

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:19.880
Our project, GECCO,
is code-driven managed

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:23.510
IT infrastructure for Alphabet
entities and acquisitions

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:25.280
deployed in Google Cloud.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:26.780
The problem that
we set out to solve

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:30.140
was consistent management of
many unrelated Active Directory

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:32.500
domains rather than
a single large forest

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:34.700
as Kenny was describing.

00:30:34.700 --> 00:30:36.320
With a fairly small
team, we manage

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:40.130
services like Active
Directory, DNS, DHTP, MPS,

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:45.110
and Radius for many small to
medium-sized environments.

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:46.840
In order to
accomplish this, we've

00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:49.130
put tremendous focus on
using the DevOps approach

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:50.810
of infrastructure as code.

00:30:50.810 --> 00:30:53.810
Using code and automation
to deploy and maintain

00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:58.350
these environments and
minimizing manual changes.

00:30:58.350 --> 00:31:01.410
So I'd like to share some
of the key design goals that

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:03.395
informed the design of GECCO.

00:31:03.395 --> 00:31:05.520
And I propose that these
are goals that most of you

00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:07.311
would share if you were
building out domain

00:31:07.311 --> 00:31:08.844
infrastructure in Google Cloud.

00:31:08.844 --> 00:31:10.260
I'll give you some
examples of how

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:12.270
we got to where we are,
but just understand

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:15.010
there are many other ways
to achieve the same goals.

00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:18.340
So let me break each
one of these down.

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:20.410
First, it's important
to limit access

00:31:20.410 --> 00:31:23.650
to the GCP products that contain
your domain infrastructure.

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:25.630
Protecting your
domain controllers

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:27.220
from malicious or
accidental action

00:31:27.220 --> 00:31:29.930
is critical to the security
of these environments.

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:31.390
So we build domain
infrastructure

00:31:31.390 --> 00:31:33.970
in isolated GCP projects.

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:37.000
Having ownership or
instance admin roles in GCE

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:39.460
means having tremendous
power over the instances that

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:42.050
run in those projects.

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:44.620
For example, depending on
your IAM roles in the project,

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:46.510
you can create or
delete instances,

00:31:46.510 --> 00:31:49.960
you can access their logs,
create new Windows accounts,

00:31:49.960 --> 00:31:54.170
and change passwords
on those machines.

00:31:54.170 --> 00:31:56.170
So we as role accounts
and custom IAM

00:31:56.170 --> 00:31:58.000
grants to give just
the access needed

00:31:58.000 --> 00:31:59.770
to resources in our projects.

00:31:59.770 --> 00:32:02.080
We only allow folks
that are domain admins

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:05.170
in the environment to become
instance admins in the projects

00:32:05.170 --> 00:32:07.457
that they run in.

00:32:07.457 --> 00:32:09.040
We carefully consider
the consequences

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:11.770
of granting even lesser
access to compute resources

00:32:11.770 --> 00:32:15.450
in the projects that contain
these domain controllers.

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:19.290
Approach every Cloud IAM grant
by asking yourself, can you--

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:23.220
can someone use this access
to escalate their privileges

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:24.300
in the domain?

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:28.230
Will this grant let someone
modify instances, metadata,

00:32:28.230 --> 00:32:30.630
or access sensitive logs?

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:33.600
So now, we built our domain
controllers in an isolated

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:35.530
project, fantastic.

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:37.200
They're running
perfectly, smoothly.

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:39.116
Nothing's is going wrong,
they're under glass,

00:32:39.116 --> 00:32:40.630
and nobody can talk to them.

00:32:40.630 --> 00:32:43.510
So what do we do next?

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:47.565
First, we use instance tags to
define VPC firewall rules that

00:32:47.565 --> 00:32:50.657
control the flow of network
traffic within our project.

00:32:50.657 --> 00:32:52.740
This ensures that we know
where all of our traffic

00:32:52.740 --> 00:32:55.586
is coming from and
where it is going to.

00:32:55.586 --> 00:32:56.960
Once these rules
are in place, we

00:32:56.960 --> 00:32:58.335
can establish
network connections

00:32:58.335 --> 00:33:03.240
to our customers' cloud
and on-prem environments.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:06.270
We want our infrastructure to
be available and performant,

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:08.080
so we do a couple of things.

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:10.710
We co-locate our instances
in the same GCP zones

00:33:10.710 --> 00:33:12.870
that our customer
projects run in.

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:15.090
And then we use
Cloud VPC Peering

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:16.650
to create fast
network connections

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:18.390
between the virtual
private cloud

00:33:18.390 --> 00:33:22.240
networks of these projects.

00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:25.410
Finally, we interact with
on-prem networks via cloud VPN,

00:33:25.410 --> 00:33:28.530
again, placing our compute
instances in cloud regions that

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:29.730
are close to those network.

00:33:33.700 --> 00:33:35.950
Our next design goal was
to automate provisioning

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:38.080
and to implement
source-controlled configuration

00:33:38.080 --> 00:33:39.100
management.

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:43.190
Infrastructure as code is key
to scaling IT as a service.

00:33:43.190 --> 00:33:45.190
But we're not just
concerned with shaving down

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:46.600
provisioning time.

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:49.330
By centralizing our code,
and config, and source

00:33:49.330 --> 00:33:51.310
repositories, we can
force all changes

00:33:51.310 --> 00:33:54.190
to go through peer review
and a suite of tests.

00:33:54.190 --> 00:33:57.640
And with sufficient provisioning
and configuration automation,

00:33:57.640 --> 00:33:59.620
you might choose to build
new instances rather

00:33:59.620 --> 00:34:01.480
than applying updates
to existing ones,

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:03.094
as Kenny described.

00:34:03.094 --> 00:34:04.510
You can bring new
instances online

00:34:04.510 --> 00:34:06.801
and ensure they are operating
correctly before bringing

00:34:06.801 --> 00:34:10.094
your existing capacity offline.

00:34:10.094 --> 00:34:12.260
And with GECCO, our instance
provisioning automation

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:14.449
is built around getting our
configuration management

00:34:14.449 --> 00:34:17.510
in place as early as
possible and making sure

00:34:17.510 --> 00:34:19.130
that it stays in control.

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:21.320
So we automate deployment
of new domain controllers

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:26.280
and other instance types
using a couple of stages.

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:28.386
So for the first stage
of our instance build,

00:34:28.386 --> 00:34:30.260
we take advantage of
the CIS prep specialized

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:34.010
stage of Windows installation
to inject our build artifacts

00:34:34.010 --> 00:34:35.210
onto the machine.

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:37.280
This is possible
thanks to the GCE agent

00:34:37.280 --> 00:34:39.949
that runs in this
stock GCE image

00:34:39.949 --> 00:34:41.935
that we build our instance from.

00:34:41.935 --> 00:34:44.060
It will look for a script
configured in the project

00:34:44.060 --> 00:34:47.239
or instance metadata, and
then it will run that script.

00:34:47.239 --> 00:34:48.830
All we are doing in
this initial stage

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:52.469
is to download build artifacts
from a canonical source.

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:55.580
So here I've highlighted a
very short PowerShell script

00:34:55.580 --> 00:34:56.900
where I'm storing--

00:34:56.900 --> 00:34:59.192
that I'm storing as a
value in a hash table.

00:34:59.192 --> 00:35:01.400
And when I pass that hash
table to the command below,

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:05.370
the key value pairs in the hash
table become instance metadata.

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:06.920
So when I provisioned
the machine,

00:35:06.920 --> 00:35:10.010
the GCE agent will go looking
for the specialized script

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.000
and metadata.

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:13.809
And upon finding it,
it will execute it.

00:35:13.809 --> 00:35:15.350
As I said, all this
particular script

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:19.250
is doing is copying instance
build artifacts from GCS.

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:21.930
Now, I could, instead of
using a Git repository,

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:24.897
or I could be using a package
repository, or something else.

00:35:24.897 --> 00:35:26.480
The important thing
is that all of the

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:28.340
build artifacts
that I'm using are

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:29.729
coming from a vetted source.

00:35:29.729 --> 00:35:31.520
Everything I'm using
to build this instance

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:33.180
should be centrally stored.

00:35:33.180 --> 00:35:35.180
Everything that I'm using
to build this instance

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:37.430
should be peer
reviewed or should

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:39.740
be built by something that
was itself peer reviewed.

00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:44.730
After the CIS prep
specialized stage completes

00:35:44.730 --> 00:35:47.249
and the instance reboots,
our second stage script

00:35:47.249 --> 00:35:47.790
is triggered.

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:50.310
Now, this script will install
configuration management

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:53.010
prerequisites, initialize
the configuration management

00:35:53.010 --> 00:35:55.596
system, and then shepherd
it through its first run

00:35:55.596 --> 00:35:56.970
of config management
to make sure

00:35:56.970 --> 00:35:59.070
that it completes successfully.

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:01.950
For our domain controllers, we
use PowerShell Desired State

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:04.260
Configuration as our
configuration management

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:05.950
system.

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:07.740
So here I've given
you an example

00:36:07.740 --> 00:36:09.510
of the DSC
configuration required

00:36:09.510 --> 00:36:12.390
to create a brand new domain
controller on a Windows Server

00:36:12.390 --> 00:36:13.740
Core machine.

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:15.570
That's really about it.

00:36:15.570 --> 00:36:18.960
The WindowsFeature DSC resource
ensures that the AD Domain

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:22.350
Services feature is installed
and the XAD domain resource

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:24.362
handles the creation
of the domain.

00:36:24.362 --> 00:36:25.820
In a production
environment, you're

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:28.111
going to see a lot of additional
code and configuration

00:36:28.111 --> 00:36:30.839
to handle things like credential
management, and testing,

00:36:30.839 --> 00:36:33.130
and other things that are
specifically our environment.

00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:36.090
But this really is all there is
to creating a new domain using

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:37.020
PowerShell DSC.

00:36:39.374 --> 00:36:40.790
Speaking of
credential management,

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.290
the final thing I
wanted to touch on

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:43.949
was protecting your secrets.

00:36:43.949 --> 00:36:45.740
Building domain
infrastructure necessitates

00:36:45.740 --> 00:36:47.870
handling some very important
credentials, including

00:36:47.870 --> 00:36:50.150
those you needed to create
a new domain controller,

00:36:50.150 --> 00:36:53.045
or a new domain entirely.

00:36:53.045 --> 00:36:54.920
Everyone at some point
is going to be tempted

00:36:54.920 --> 00:36:57.170
to put a password in a script.

00:36:57.170 --> 00:36:59.330
We've all been around
the block, don't do it.

00:36:59.330 --> 00:37:00.590
Don't use plain text.

00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:01.940
Don't just use obfuscation.

00:37:01.940 --> 00:37:03.590
Rot13 is not encryption.

00:37:03.590 --> 00:37:05.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:06.980
Really encrypt your secrets.

00:37:06.980 --> 00:37:09.470
Store them in a place where
you will have an audit trail

00:37:09.470 --> 00:37:11.270
every time they are accessed.

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:12.770
And have sufficient
logging in place

00:37:12.770 --> 00:37:14.810
to know when those
credentials are used anywhere

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:15.890
in your environment.

00:37:15.890 --> 00:37:17.690
So there are a lot of ways
to protect your credentials,

00:37:17.690 --> 00:37:19.981
but I wanted to show you a
pretty simple example that's

00:37:19.981 --> 00:37:22.770
enabled by Cloud Key Management
Service, or Cloud KMS.

00:37:25.620 --> 00:37:28.350
Cloud KMS allows you to
encrypt and decrypt secrets

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:31.200
based on Cloud
IAM access grants.

00:37:31.200 --> 00:37:32.640
So, I can have one
set of accounts

00:37:32.640 --> 00:37:34.740
that has permissions
just to encrypt secrets,

00:37:34.740 --> 00:37:37.114
and then I can have another
set of accounts that just has

00:37:37.114 --> 00:37:39.810
permission to decrypt secrets.

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:41.880
Here I'm using a very
simple gcloud command

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:44.070
to encrypt a plain
text string and store

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:45.390
the results in a file.

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:48.130
Then I'm uploading that file
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:50.130
And when I need to use
this very secure password

00:37:50.130 --> 00:37:53.040
to join my domain
controller to my domain,

00:37:53.040 --> 00:37:56.160
the code can retrieve the
encrypted file from GCS,

00:37:56.160 --> 00:37:58.920
decrypt the secret, and inject
it into my configuration

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:01.890
management without the plain
text password ever landing

00:38:01.890 --> 00:38:02.390
on disk.

00:38:05.240 --> 00:38:08.586
Now, we could, if you imagine,
just take things a step further

00:38:08.586 --> 00:38:10.210
and build a process
where no human ever

00:38:10.210 --> 00:38:12.530
has to know what
certain passwords are.

00:38:12.530 --> 00:38:14.530
So you can imagine having
a process to generate

00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:17.170
a new random password as part
of an automated provisioning

00:38:17.170 --> 00:38:18.200
process.

00:38:18.200 --> 00:38:20.950
So let's say that I have a
password for a break glass

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:22.357
administrative account.

00:38:22.357 --> 00:38:24.190
The right service account
in the right place

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:26.080
will be able to access
this secret in order

00:38:26.080 --> 00:38:28.630
to set the password for
the break glass account.

00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:31.090
But no human will ever
have to know what this is.

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:34.520
If OnCall uses Cloud KMS
to decrypt the secret,

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:36.769
I'll know about it
thanks to my audit logs.

00:38:36.769 --> 00:38:38.560
And the same process
can be triggered again

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:40.120
to automatically
rotate the password.

00:38:42.950 --> 00:38:46.490
So in summary, when building
domain infrastructure in GCP,

00:38:46.490 --> 00:38:49.820
you should carefully consider
these aspects of your design--

00:38:49.820 --> 00:38:51.710
limiting access to
just where it is needed

00:38:51.710 --> 00:38:53.840
and isolating your
domain infrastructure,

00:38:53.840 --> 00:38:55.832
automating your
provisioning and deployment

00:38:55.832 --> 00:38:57.290
and using configuration
management,

00:38:57.290 --> 00:38:59.637
and protecting your
critical secrets.

00:38:59.637 --> 00:39:00.470
Thank you very much.

00:39:00.470 --> 00:39:02.261
I'll hand the rest of
the time back to Sid.

00:39:02.261 --> 00:39:04.937
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:07.292 --> 00:39:10.030
SIDDHARTH BHAI: Thank you
Ben so much for joining us.

00:39:10.030 --> 00:39:12.010
The two key takeaways I
think from this segment

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:15.550
for me were, one is like
an operational step-by-step

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:17.440
of how can you
actually automate DC

00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:19.217
build using tooling,
which is hopefully

00:39:19.217 --> 00:39:20.800
familiar to a lot
of you who have been

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:22.720
working with AD for a while.

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:25.180
Second was you saw Ben talk
about what's a good way

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:27.910
to go ahead and protect secrets
in the cloud in this case,

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:29.710
for the Active
Directory use case,

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:33.347
but using Cloud KMS and GCS
during the encrypted secret,

00:39:33.347 --> 00:39:35.680
it's something you can extend
for other applications you

00:39:35.680 --> 00:39:37.870
do with GCP, too.

00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:39.910
As we go into this final
section of the talk,

00:39:39.910 --> 00:39:42.160
I wanted to go ahead and
touch upon and bring together

00:39:42.160 --> 00:39:43.720
some of these best practices.

00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:45.710
This is obviously
not a complete list.

00:39:45.710 --> 00:39:47.650
You've heard some very
great pearls of wisdom

00:39:47.650 --> 00:39:49.099
from both Ben and Kenny.

00:39:49.099 --> 00:39:50.890
But thought I'd leave
you with a few things

00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:52.990
to keep in mind as
you start thinking

00:39:52.990 --> 00:39:55.270
about running Active
Directory or extending it

00:39:55.270 --> 00:39:57.850
to Google Cloud.

00:39:57.850 --> 00:40:00.559
The first set of things is
around Active Directory design.

00:40:00.559 --> 00:40:02.350
So one of the things
you should think about

00:40:02.350 --> 00:40:04.690
as you move to cloud
is, there's no reason

00:40:04.690 --> 00:40:06.960
to not be highly available.

00:40:06.960 --> 00:40:08.800
So for domain
controllers, always

00:40:08.800 --> 00:40:12.490
have at least two
DCs, one in each zone.

00:40:12.490 --> 00:40:15.320
Second, when you think about
your Active Directory site

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:17.690
topology, you have two options.

00:40:17.690 --> 00:40:19.660
One is you can pick a
simple option, which

00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:23.800
is you create a single Active
Directory site for all of GCP.

00:40:23.800 --> 00:40:26.620
And using the power of
our multi-regional VPC,

00:40:26.620 --> 00:40:28.750
you're effectively able
to have that work well.

00:40:28.750 --> 00:40:31.070
It's a quick way to get started.

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:33.410
Second, you heard Kenny
describe, especially as you

00:40:33.410 --> 00:40:35.630
move towards more
performant applications,

00:40:35.630 --> 00:40:38.240
to have one site per region.

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:41.449
And what this will do is for any
of the applications or servers

00:40:41.449 --> 00:40:43.490
you have running in that
region, they will always

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:45.770
go to the closest
domain controller

00:40:45.770 --> 00:40:48.490
and you'll see low latencies.

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:50.770
In terms of connectivity
back to on-premises

00:40:50.770 --> 00:40:53.579
there's networking options,
Cloud VPN, Cloud Interconnect.

00:40:53.579 --> 00:40:55.120
You're welcome to
go study those more

00:40:55.120 --> 00:40:56.990
and see which one
works well for you.

00:40:56.990 --> 00:40:59.380
That's because a lot of
Active Directory operations

00:40:59.380 --> 00:41:01.882
require non-internet,
like private network,

00:41:01.882 --> 00:41:04.090
line-of-sight connectivity
between domain controllers

00:41:04.090 --> 00:41:06.680
as many of you are
probably aware.

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:08.800
The last part is, for any
applications or servers

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:11.170
you choose to move
to GCP, and you

00:41:11.170 --> 00:41:14.590
do have one or more AD
sites on GCP, placing them

00:41:14.590 --> 00:41:16.349
in the same Active
Directory site

00:41:16.349 --> 00:41:17.890
will lead to the
right type of things

00:41:17.890 --> 00:41:19.900
happening, which should
keep your DNS and AD

00:41:19.900 --> 00:41:24.190
operations working well,
finding the closest DC.

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:26.330
For an operations
and security angle,

00:41:26.330 --> 00:41:28.110
a few things to keep in mind.

00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:30.260
One is there's obviously
a plethora of choices

00:41:30.260 --> 00:41:32.182
when you come to cloud
for running Compute.

00:41:32.182 --> 00:41:33.890
You obviously want to
make, just like you

00:41:33.890 --> 00:41:35.960
would if you were
purchasing hardware,

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:39.400
to run your domain controllers
make the right type of choices.

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:42.020
Preemptable VMs may be cool,
but probably not a good idea

00:41:42.020 --> 00:41:43.915
for running AD
domain controllers.

00:41:43.915 --> 00:41:47.870
You want to think about static
IPs and things like that.

00:41:47.870 --> 00:41:50.060
In terms of GCP IM
permissions, you

00:41:50.060 --> 00:41:52.917
saw that being something
we covered in last two case

00:41:52.917 --> 00:41:55.170
studies, but I did want
to hone in the point.

00:41:55.170 --> 00:41:57.140
Here's something
to keep in mind.

00:41:57.140 --> 00:42:02.650
If you have a domain controller
running in a VM on Compute,

00:42:02.650 --> 00:42:06.610
anybody who has Compute Instance
admin on that GCP project

00:42:06.610 --> 00:42:09.490
can go right click,
reset Windows password,

00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:12.880
and effectively elevate
themselves to a domain admin.

00:42:12.880 --> 00:42:15.610
Now just like you wouldn't
very loosely put your Active

00:42:15.610 --> 00:42:17.530
Directory on-premises
domain controllers

00:42:17.530 --> 00:42:20.080
in physical environments
you didn't fully trust,

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:22.900
when it comes to managing it in
the cloud, especially in GCP,

00:42:22.900 --> 00:42:25.740
you want to be mindful of
which type of IM permissions

00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:27.820
you have on those projects.

00:42:27.820 --> 00:42:30.850
In particular, anybody
with a set IM policy

00:42:30.850 --> 00:42:34.240
is able to set IM permissions
on that GCP project

00:42:34.240 --> 00:42:36.700
and give themselves or
other a variety of IM roles.

00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:38.950
So it's generally a good
hygiene to keep in mind,

00:42:38.950 --> 00:42:41.200
but especially when you're
running domain controllers,

00:42:41.200 --> 00:42:43.210
be mindful of that.

00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:45.160
Second, we have a
resource hierarchy

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:47.729
within GCP, which is beyond
the scope of this talk today.

00:42:47.729 --> 00:42:49.270
But to give you an
idea, you can have

00:42:49.270 --> 00:42:52.000
projects, which are inside
of folders, which are inside

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:53.492
of organizations.

00:42:53.492 --> 00:42:55.700
And so there is a inheritance
model of IM permissions

00:42:55.700 --> 00:42:56.230
there too.

00:42:56.230 --> 00:42:57.590
So I see several of you nodding.

00:42:57.590 --> 00:42:58.570
I won't go into more detail.

00:42:58.570 --> 00:43:00.730
But something to study
and keep in mind as you're

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:03.200
thinking of running AD.

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:05.920
Lastly, if you wish to
have more isolation for AD,

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:08.441
you can always set up a Bastian
Active Directory Project.

00:43:08.441 --> 00:43:10.440
It is more things that
you'll have to configure,

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:13.180
but I did want you to know that
is an option for high security

00:43:13.180 --> 00:43:14.170
cases.

00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:17.740
You can then still use VPC
Peering and shared VPCs

00:43:17.740 --> 00:43:20.380
to have all your domain
controllers in that project,

00:43:20.380 --> 00:43:22.426
but have your servers
and applications

00:43:22.426 --> 00:43:23.800
and other projects
that can still

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:26.080
reach those domain controllers.

00:43:26.080 --> 00:43:28.480
Lastly, there is a
session later this week--

00:43:28.480 --> 00:43:30.350
I have a link in a
couple of slides--

00:43:30.350 --> 00:43:32.980
which we'll talk about how
you can use hardened security

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:35.560
on Google Cloud Platform for
all of your sensitive workloads,

00:43:35.560 --> 00:43:36.550
including AD.

00:43:36.550 --> 00:43:38.650
So I encourage you to
find out more about that

00:43:38.650 --> 00:43:39.733
and look to leverage that.

00:43:41.599 --> 00:43:43.640
So if all of this is
sounding interesting to you,

00:43:43.640 --> 00:43:45.223
and you when it gets
started, I wanted

00:43:45.223 --> 00:43:47.930
to leave you with just a
few quick slides of examples

00:43:47.930 --> 00:43:49.506
of things you can do.

00:43:49.506 --> 00:43:52.460
One is we have a Google
Cloud solution that talks you

00:43:52.460 --> 00:43:54.130
through step by
step how you can get

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:57.726
your highly available Active
Directory domain up on GCP.

00:43:57.726 --> 00:43:59.600
I have a slide with all
the links in a minute

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:01.820
if you want to take
a photo of that.

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:05.140
Second is, you can also choose
to extend an existing AD

00:44:05.140 --> 00:44:06.380
domain onto GCP.

00:44:06.380 --> 00:44:07.490
So there is a white paper.

00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:10.280
Phase three of this white paper
talks about Active Directory

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:13.230
considerations.

00:44:13.230 --> 00:44:14.499
There is a click to deploy.

00:44:14.499 --> 00:44:17.040
If you wanted to go ahead and
just get and AD domain spun up,

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:19.248
and start playing with it,
and experimenting with it,

00:44:19.248 --> 00:44:22.397
we have that, something you
can go ahead and leverage.

00:44:22.397 --> 00:44:23.980
And a lot of what
you have heard today

00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:25.270
is focused around two themes.

00:44:25.270 --> 00:44:28.390
One is having high available,
multi-regional Active Directory

00:44:28.390 --> 00:44:29.560
on GCP.

00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:32.050
And B has been
around specifically

00:44:32.050 --> 00:44:34.870
automating domain
controller creation.

00:44:34.870 --> 00:44:37.480
And so for that, I'm actually
very excited to share with you

00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:38.590
